<!--
  PP24: what Visual Studio opens.

  The tree is CMakeLists.txt plus a vcpkg.json, which Visual Studio can open as a CMake folder -
  and a folder is not a solution: no project references, no NuGet, and no F5 with a managed
  debugger attached to the code being written. This is the solution the managed half of the port is
  written inside rather than beside.

  Why .slnx and not .sln. The XML form is what the .NET 10 SDK's `dotnet new sln` now produces, and
  the classic format's blocks of GUIDs are a merge conflict waiting for the first branch that adds
  a project - which this port will do repeatedly as Block F splits the core into components. The
  cost is tooling older than Visual Studio 2022 17.10, and this project targets net10.0-windows, so
  nothing that can build it is that old.

  The native half is deliberately absent. lib/ and the Qt client are CMake and stay CMake -
  compile.cmd remains the only build for them, and it is what produces the chiaki-shim.dll this
  solution's project loads. Whatever of Block F ends up staying native is consumed as a binary or
  as a vcxproj this file references, which is a thing a solution does well; inventing that entry
  before there is anything behind it would be a reference to nothing.
-->
<Solution>
  <Folder Name="/app/">
    <Project Path="app/ChiakiNg.csproj" />
  </Folder>
  <!-- PP35. The test project holds no expected values: it drives the same vector readers the
       host's own selftest does, so the C suite stays the single oracle and this adds only
       granularity, one xUnit case per recorded case rather than one assertion over all of them.
       (The switch that runs that selftest is spelled with two leading dashes, which an XML
       comment cannot contain - this file refuses to parse with it written out.) -->
  <Folder Name="/tests/">
    <Project Path="tests/ChiakiNg.Tests/ChiakiNg.Tests.csproj" />
  </Folder>
  <!-- PP436. The site's front page and llms.txt name this executable and say what it prints, and
       nothing in this repo built it: seven first-party csproj files, and the solution held two. A
       promise the copy makes about a program is gated now that compile.cmd reaches it.

       PP570: AND measure-startup, which was the unexplained one. This comment said four stayed out
       and gave reasons for three; the fourth ships a self-test flag that no gate could run, because
       no gate built it - PP569's gap one project over, and worse, since compare-baselines was at
       least compiled. It carries TreatWarningsAsErrors, declared before it was added here for
       PP438's reason: the solution decides who that policy binds, and PP436 added the other tool
       without it.

       The four that stay out, each with its reason: spike/present-path, spike/video-upscale and
       spike/frame-generation are records of an experiment's answer rather than live code, and
       gate/alloc-budget's answer already migrated into AllocBudgetTests. None is named on the site,
       and none ships an assertion of its own. -->
  <Folder Name="/tools/">
    <Project Path="tools/compare-baselines/CompareBaselines.csproj" />
    <Project Path="tools/measure-startup/MeasureStartup.csproj" />
  </Folder>
</Solution>
